 + \\frac{B}{x-5},\n\\]\nwe start by factoring the denominator on the left-hand side:\n\\[\nx^2 - 8x + 15 = (x-3)(x-5).\n\\]\nThus, the equation becomes\n\\[\n\\frac{4x}{(x-3)(x-5)} = \\frac{A}{x-3} + \\frac{B}{x-5}.\n\\]\nNext, we combine the right-hand side over a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{A}{x-3} + \\frac{B}{x-5} = \\frac{A(x-5) + B(x-3)}{(x-3)(x-5)}.\n\\]\nSince the denominators are the same, we equate the numerators:\n\\[\n4x = A(x-5) + B(x-3).\n\\]\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\\[\n4x = Ax - 5A + Bx - 3B.\n\\]\nCombining like terms, we have:\n\\[\n4x = (A + B)x - (5A + 3B).\n\\]\nFor the equation to hold for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms on both sides must be equal. Therefore, we set up the following system of equations:\n\\[\nA + B = 4,\n\\]\n\\[\n-5A - 3B = 0.\n\\]\nWe solve this system step by step. First, solve the second equation for \\(B\\):\n\\[\n-5A - 3B = 0 \\implies -3B = 5A \\implies B = -\\frac{5}{3}A.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(B = -\\frac{5}{3}A\\) into the first equation:\n\\[\nA + \\left(-\\frac{5}{3}A\\right) = 4 \\implies A - \\frac{5}{3}A = 4 \\implies \\frac{3A - 5A}{3} = 4 \\implies -\\frac{2A}{3} = 4.\n\\]\nSolving for \\(A\\), we get:\n\\[\n-\\frac{2A}{3} = 4 \\implies -2A = 12 \\implies A = -6.\n\\]\nNow substitute \\(A = -6\\) back into \\(B = -\\frac{5}{3}A\\):\n\\[\nB = -\\frac{5}{3}(-6) = 10.\n\\]\nThus, the values of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are \\(A = -6\\) and \\(B = 10\\). Therefore, the ordered pair \\((A, B)\\) is\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-6, 10)}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(-6, 10)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:147"}
